Article 6 During the trial of an enforcement objection lawsuit filed by a person other than a party, if the enforcement applicant requests the people's court to continue enforcement and provides corresponding security, the people's court responsible for hearing the enforcement objection lawsuit shall rule on whether to permit such continuation. Where the enforcement court continues enforcement according to law, the enforcement objection lawsuit filed by the person other than a party shall be handled separately according to the following circumstances:
(1) If the person other than a party does not enjoy a civil right or interest sufficient to exclude compulsory enforcement over the subject matter of enforcement, the claim shall be dismissed by judgment;
(2) If the person other than a party enjoys a civil right or interest sufficient to exclude compulsory enforcement over the subject matter of enforcement, and the subject matter of enforcement has been transferred to the enforcement applicant through enforcement procedures such as auction or debt offset, the judgment shall order that the subject matter of enforcement shall not be enforced, and shall revoke the relevant auction or debt offset ruling; if the subject matter has already been delivered to the enforcement applicant, the judgment shall at the same time order the enforcement applicant to return it; if the enforcement applicant refuses to return it, compulsory execution shall be carried out;
(3) If the person other than a party enjoys a civil right or interest sufficient to exclude compulsory enforcement over the subject matter of enforcement, and the subject matter of enforcement has been lawfully acquired by another person through enforcement procedures such as auction or sale by conversion, the judgment shall order that the conversion proceeds of the subject matter of enforcement shall not be enforced, and the enforcement court shall distribute the conversion proceeds to the person other than a party; if the conversion proceeds have already been distributed to the enforcement applicant or the remaining conversion proceeds have been refunded to the person subject to enforcement, the judgment shall at the same time order the enforcement applicant and the person subject to enforcement to return them; if they refuse to return them, compulsory execution shall be carried out; if the enforcement court explains to the person other than a party that the subject matter of enforcement has been lawfully acquired by another person, but the person other than a party refuses to accept the conversion proceeds, the conversion proceeds shall be deposited, and the person other than a party shall be informed that he or she may collect them at any time within five years from the date of deposit.
Under the circumstances specified in items (2) and (3) of the preceding paragraph, if the person other than a party considers that the enforcement applicant's request to continue enforcement is erroneous and has caused losses to him or her, he or she may separately assert rights against the enforcement applicant, the enforcement guarantor, or others according to law.

Article 11 Where a people's court carries out compulsory enforcement on a new commercial housing registered under the name of a real estate development enterprise subject to enforcement, and a person other than a party, on the ground that he or she is a consumer of commercial housing, files an enforcement objection lawsuit requesting to exclude compulsory enforcement of the priority right of construction project price payment, mortgage, and ordinary monetary claims, and can prove that his or her claim simultaneously meets the following conditions, the people's court shall support the claim:
(1) Before the seizure, the person other than a party had signed a lawful and valid written sales contract with the real estate development enterprise or others;
(2) Before the seizure, the person other than a party had paid the full price, or had paid part of the price in accordance with the contract and, after the seizure but before the conclusion of the court debate at first instance, had delivered the remaining price to the people's court for enforcement;
(3) The purchased commercial housing is used to meet the living needs of the family.
If the person other than a party files a lawsuit requesting the person subject to enforcement to go through the formalities for the transfer of ownership registration of the commercial housing, and the conditions specified in the preceding paragraph are met, the people's court shall support the request according to law.
If the people's court renders a judgment dismissing the claim of the person other than a party, the remaining price delivered by the person other than a party for enforcement shall be promptly refunded.

Article 14 Where a people's court carries out compulsory enforcement on immovable property registered under the name of the person subject to enforcement, and a person other than a party, on the ground that he or she is the purchaser of the immovable property, files an enforcement objection lawsuit requesting to exclude compulsory enforcement of ordinary monetary claims, and can prove that his or her claim simultaneously meets the following conditions, the people's court shall support the claim:
(1) Before the seizure, the person other than a party had signed a lawful and valid written sales contract with the person subject to enforcement;
(2) Before the seizure, the person other than a party had paid the full price, or had paid part of the price in accordance with the contract and, after the seizure but before the conclusion of the court debate at first instance, had delivered the remaining price to the people's court for enforcement;
(3) Before the seizure, the person other than a party had lawfully occupied the immovable property;
(4) The failure to undergo the transfer registration of the ownership of the immovable property is not attributable to reasons on the part of the person other than a party.
If the people's court renders a judgment dismissing the claim of the person other than a party, the remaining price delivered by the person other than a party for enforcement shall be promptly refunded.

Article 15 Where a people's court carries out compulsory enforcement on immovable property registered under the name of the person subject to enforcement, and a person other than a party, on the ground that the person subject to enforcement has used the immovable property to offset debts owed to him or her, files an enforcement objection lawsuit requesting to exclude compulsory enforcement of ordinary monetary claims, and can prove that his or her claim simultaneously meets the following conditions, the people's court shall support the claim:
(1) A genuine creditor-debtor relationship exists between the person other than a party and the person subject to enforcement, and the debt performance period has expired; before the seizure, the person other than a party had signed a lawful and valid agreement with the person subject to enforcement to offset the debt using the immovable property;
(2) Evidence shows that the amount of the debt offset is essentially equivalent to the actual value of the subject matter of enforcement at the time of the offset;
(3) Before the seizure, the person other than a party had lawfully occupied the immovable property;
(4) The failure to undergo the transfer registration of the ownership of the immovable property is not attributable to reasons on the part of the person other than a party.

Article 16 Before the seizure by the people's court, any of the following circumstances may be found to constitute 'not attributable to reasons on the part of the person other than a party' as referred to in Articles 14 and 15 of this Interpretation:
(1) The person other than a party and the person subject to enforcement have jointly submitted an application to the immovable property registration authority for the transfer registration of ownership;
(2) The person other than a party has requested the person subject to enforcement to perform contractual obligations such as going through the formalities for the transfer registration of ownership, or a dispute has arisen with the person subject to enforcement concerning the transfer registration of ownership and a lawsuit has been filed or arbitration has been applied for, etc.;
(3) The newly constructed commercial housing does not yet meet the conditions for initial registration;
(4) The online contract registration (net signing and filing) for the sales contract has been completed;
(5) The person subject to enforcement or others have notified the person other than a party to undergo the transfer registration of ownership of the immovable property, but the person other than a party has not been dilatory in doing so;
(6) Other circumstances not attributable to reasons on the part of the person other than a party.
